# Database and migration rules

## Every table

    id             BIGINT UNSIGNED AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY   internal only
    public_id      CHAR(26) NOT NULL UNIQUE                     ULID, used in URLs
    clinic_id      BIGINT UNSIGNED NOT NULL                     on every clinical table
    created_at     DATETIME(6) NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP(6)
    updated_at     DATETIME(6) NOT NULL ... ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP(6)
    created_by_id  BIGINT UNSIGNED NULL -> user.id
    updated_by_id  BIGINT UNSIGNED NULL -> user.id
    deleted_at     DATETIME(6) NULL                             soft delete
    deleted_by_id  BIGINT UNSIGNED NULL
    version        INT UNSIGNED NOT NULL DEFAULT 1              optimistic locking

Get these from the mixins. Do not retype them.

## Types

- Engine InnoDB, charset utf8mb4, collation utf8mb4_0900_ai_ci. Use
  utf8mb4_0900_as_cs where case matters (codes, token hashes).
- Timestamps stored UTC in DATETIME(6). Inherently local dates (birth date,
  surgery date) are DATE.
- Money: DECIMAL(12,3) plus a currency column. Never FLOAT or DOUBLE.
- Clinical measurements: SMALLINT or DECIMAL with an explicit unit. Never float
  where exactness matters.
- Enum-like values: VARCHAR with a CHECK constraint, or a lookup table when
  clinicians may extend the list. Never MySQL ENUM (altering it locks the table
  and it cannot be translated).
- Laterality: VARCHAR(10) CHECK IN ('right','left','bilateral','midline','na').
  A bilateral finding on a paired structure is stored as TWO rows, one per side.
- JSON only for genuinely schemaless payloads (device raw export, template
  placeholder map). If you will filter, sort or aggregate on it, make it a
  column.

## Keys and indexes

- Never expose the integer `id`. URLs and API payloads use `public_id`.
- Every tenant-scoped index starts with `clinic_id`.
- Index names: ix_<table>__<cols>, uq_<table>__<cols>, fk_<table>__<ref>.
- Foreign keys are ON DELETE RESTRICT by default. Clinical rows are never
  removed by a cascade.
- Timeline and list queries need a covering index on
  (clinic_id, patient_id, <date> DESC).

## Where a new clinical field goes

Ask this before adding a column:

- Is it a clinician-extensible finding, sparse across patients, with a
  terminology concept behind it?  -> it is an `observation` row, NOT a column.
- Is it dense, fixed-shape, high-volume numeric data (audiogram thresholds,
  questionnaire answers, prick test results)? -> its own narrow table.
- Is it an attribute of the record itself (performed_at, equipment, status,
  a computed summary metric)? -> a column on that table.

Never add a wide examination table with dozens of nullable finding columns.
That decision is argued in the architecture document and is settled.

## Migrations

- One Alembic migration per change. Review autogenerate by hand; it misses
  index and constraint intent, CHECK constraints and column comments.
- Additive first, always, so a deploy never locks a clinic out mid-morning:
  add the column nullable, backfill in a separate data migration, enforce
  NOT NULL in a later migration.
- Every migration is reversible, or is explicitly marked irreversible with a
  written reason in the docstring.
- Data migrations live in their own migration files and are idempotent.
- Never edit a migration that has been applied anywhere beyond your machine.
  Write a new one.
- Test the migration against an empty database and against a snapshot of the
  previous release's schema before proposing it.

## Reference data

Terminology, formulary, instruments, protocols and templates are seeded by
versioned scripts in `cli/seed.py`, never by hand-written SQL in a migration
and never hardcoded in Python. Applying a seed records a row in
`reference_data_version`.

## Performance

- No SELECT *. List queries select explicit columns.
- Every list query paginates.
- Declare eager loading; lazy loading in an async context is a bug waiting to
  happen.
- audit_log and access_log are partitioned by month.
- Slow query threshold 200 ms, reviewed weekly.
